GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 18(1) the requirement is a work availability requirement` MEANS
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "a 'work availability requirement' is a requirement that a claimant be available for work" (a definition)
    `it requires the claimant to be available for work`

-- (2) "'available for work' means able and willing immediately to take up paid work (or more paid work or better-paid work)".
-- s 18(5): regulations may define what is meant by being able and willing immediately; where supplied it replaces the plain test.
-- src:206 | (5) Regulations may for the purposes of subsection (2) define what is meant by a person being able and willing immediately to take up work.
@ref s 18(5) (src:206-206)
-- src:198 | (2) For the purposes of this section “available for work” means able and willing immediately to take up paid work (or more paid work or better-paid work).
@ref s 18(2) (src:198-198)
